            <div xml:id="d651">

                <pb facs="0687" n="683" sameAs="frus:frus1939v03/pg683"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.516/632: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Consul General at Shanghai</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Gauss</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1939-07-13T16:00:00-04:00">July 13, 1939—4
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>244. Chungking’s 432, July 8, 1 p.m. The Department understands that American and
                    other foreign banks voluntarily agreed in conferences with the Chinese financial
                    authorities in August, 1937 to discourage new or largely increased Chinese
                    deposits. Has this policy <pb facs="0688" n="684" xml:id="pg684"/> been
                    abandoned by foreign banks? Your comment on Chungking’s telegram under reference
                    would be appreciated.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Chungking and Peiping.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
